<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      This is only the window background Android paints before Compose draws anything. The
      real palette lives in ui/theme/Color.kt; this file exists to stop a flash of the wrong
      colour on cold start.

      ⚠ **This used to be dark for everyone, and that was a real bug by 2026-09-01.** The
      comment here said "dark only, deliberately" — true when it was written, wrong the day
      light mode shipped and became the default for a phone that states no preference. Every
      cold start in light mode began with a flash of #101422. Split into values/ and
      values-night/ on 2026-09-02, so Android picks the ground before Sika is running.

      ⚠ **The one case this cannot cover:** someone who overrides the theme *inside* Sika to
      something their phone disagrees with. Android chooses this background from the phone's
      setting, before any of Sika's code runs, so it cannot know. The splash cross-fades to
      the real theme instead of snapping — see ui/splash/SplashScreen.kt.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Sika" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/bg</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/bg</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/bg</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The system splash screen, Android 12+.

      ⚠ **Not optional, and that is the reason this style exists.** On API 31 and above
      Android shows a splash screen for every app whether it asks for one or not — Sika's was
      the default: the launcher icon on the window background. So this is not "adding a splash
      screen", it is taking control of one that was already there. Trying to replace it with
      a custom Activity instead would show BOTH, one after the other.

      ⚠ **`windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor` is doing real work.** Without it the mark is
      drawn straight onto the window background, and Sika's mark is a dark bubble — invisible
      on the dark ground in night mode. The icon background gives it its own pale disc on any
      theme, which is exactly what the attribute is for.

      No animated icon here on purpose. The system caps that animation at a second and gives
      almost no control over it; the animation Mutalib asked for runs in Compose, and this
      screen's job is only to hand over to it without a seam.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Sika.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <!--
          ⚠ **`android:`-prefixed, and the prefix is the whole point.** core-splashscreen has
          its own un-prefixed copies of these attributes for its API 21–30 backport. On API 31
          and up the *platform* draws the splash and reads the `android:` ones — so the
          un-prefixed spelling compiles, runs, and is quietly ignored. Measured on the Pixel,
          2026-09-02: the icon appeared but its background disc did not, because
          `windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor` was never being read.

          minSdk is 31, so the un-prefixed forms have nothing left to do here at all.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/bg</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor">@color/splash_icon_bg</item>
        <!-- This one IS the library's own, and has no platform equivalent. -->
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.Sika</item>
    </style>
</resources>
